{"title":"2019 AIME I Problems/Problem 12","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Proof_of_this_method\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Proof of this method</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_4\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Solution_5_(Official_MAA)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 5 (Official MAA)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Solution_6_(Dot_product)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 6 (Dot product)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_Omegalearn\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by Omegalearn</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-10\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">10</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_12&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Given <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/c/1/9c152d1f09288fc60f2486d752a679314fe856ff.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(z) = z^2-19z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"124\" height=\"19\" />, there are complex numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> with the property that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/1/301469a1d6b6bd4ddb15b26b27e22c03d5611b2e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(z)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"32\" height=\"18\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/4/6/b469183e4f8de3476e7414ac1e175d4cbaa95579.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(f(z))$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"57\" height=\"18\" /> are the vertices of a right triangle in the complex plane with a right angle at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/1/301469a1d6b6bd4ddb15b26b27e22c03d5611b2e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(z)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"32\" height=\"18\" />. There are positive integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> such that one such value of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/4/9f43af343de30bebcc0f6151b9bffcaa2c4d86bc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m+\\sqrt{n}+11i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"110\" height=\"18\" />. Find <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/d/02dbab3601fa6cce490d54263017048f69e1a35d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m+n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"51\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_12&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Notice that we must have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/8/858716158b5d3260ac8831f42aee8fcbd87f7669.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{f(f(z))-f(z)}{f(z)-z}=-\\frac{f(f(z))-f(z)}{z-f(z)}\\in i\\mathbb R .\\]\" width=\"321\" height=\"43\" />However, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/8/9/f8935addd302c0c69a1ec8711fc4ca6ba8cd90a9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(t)-t=t(t-20)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"150\" height=\"18\" />, so\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/6/e/f6e46f3299a95af8b8cb9ad12594ce6ac5710f38.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} \\frac{f(f(z))-f(z)}{f(z)-z}&amp;=\\frac{(z^2-19z)(z^2-19z-20)}{z(z-20)}\\\\ &amp;=\\frac{z(z-19)(z-20)(z+1)}{z(z-20)}\\\\ &amp;=(z-19)(z+1)\\\\ &amp;=(z-9)^2-100. \\end{align*}\" width=\"345\" height=\"145\" />\nThen, the real part of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/0/4c0d9cc6ec3009754185e813c7a9c2cff327ef50.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(z-9)^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"60\" height=\"19\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/9/e59e2c6e83eb78cca610a5fd4070ae01c8d4ae60.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$100$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" />. Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/6/096927bc3906155d61055e75ef846ec19f63ae1a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{Im}(z-9)=\\text{Im}(z)=11$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"183\" height=\"18\" />, let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/7/3/d734024fc20f47ebdb8c17161faae759aaaf5a58.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z-9=a+11i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"120\" height=\"14\" />. Then, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/4/8f4fb139cc21acb9ff46b7d7d97d94ae4014244a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[100=\\text{Re}((a+11i)^2)=a^2-121\\implies a=\\pm\\sqrt{221}.\\]\" width=\"396\" height=\"21\" />It follows that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/2/652688cffe2669d0942b5f0f076b3db01cd11430.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z=9+\\sqrt{221}+11i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"153\" height=\"18\" />, and the requested sum is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/0/d/f0d6214f9667cf038ae1f534b985d1d075eb5464.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$9+221=\\boxed{230}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"122\" height=\"23\" />.\n</p><p>(Solution by TheUltimate123)\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_12&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We will use the fact that segments <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> are perpendicular in the complex plane if and only if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/6/7/4670ce1e63a06a31873d8c904dde7005684fbec1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{a-b}{b-c}\\in i\\mathbb{R}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"85\" height=\"37\" />. To prove this, note that when dividing two complex numbers you subtract the angle of one from the other. Therefore, if the two complex numbers are perpendicular, the difference between their arguments will be 90 degrees, so subtracting the angles will yield an imaginary number with no real part(an argument of 90 degrees puts a complex number on the imaginary axis).\n</p><p>Now to apply this: \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/c/29cc2a2aaaea3af0c1b35260acb9cf8f70d1bfa7.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{f(z)-z}{f(f(z))-f(z)}\\in i\\mathbb{R}\\]\" width=\"159\" height=\"43\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/f/5df1b0913ffd02aa4cecd359fa193b132b939815.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{z^2-19z-z}{(z^2-19z)^2-19(z^2-19z)-(z^2-19z)}\\]\" width=\"312\" height=\"43\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/d/65d5d7f85b95e70c0c61506d14ee697c77cc7f9d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{z^2-20z}{z^4-38z^3+341z^2+380z}\\]\" width=\"199\" height=\"40\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/d/4/3d4a3da47b5ce7b3757ed6cd86d1155a6a8259e3.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{z(z-20)}{z(z+1)(z-19)(z-20)}\\]\" width=\"192\" height=\"43\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/7/3/473e1763fc256f757a00a3edd31c6f15075b035b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{1}{(z+1)(z-19)}\\in i\\mathbb{R}\\]\" width=\"161\" height=\"41\" />\n</p><p>The factorization of the nasty denominator above is made easier with the intuition that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/e/d/eed3eb9b0c5ef1cf357d4216e65b9f0f36bee8fd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(z-20)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"62\" height=\"18\" /> must be a divisor for the problem to lead anywhere. Now we know <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/9/d/69d23387640402aaef8b2f9e0b5ab5d7dba4122a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(z+1)(z-19)\\in i\\mathbb{R}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"159\" height=\"18\" /> so using the fact that the imaginary part of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/c/90c0a6ac26c417e2217d17f678cb3210ea9f5d83.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$11i$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"23\" height=\"13\" /> and calling the real part r, \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/e/94e627b14d85e87e791c0426d744adf8f850dcff.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(r+1+11i)(r-19+11i)\\in i\\mathbb{R}\\]\" width=\"250\" height=\"18\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/3/5/435e71966ab02af7d5e47c37b4524cda7c6f9173.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[r^2-18r-140=0\\]\" width=\"147\" height=\"15\" />\n</p><p>solving the above quadratic yields <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/9/ac90b3310247a6ef5f3dad437193366dd4697804.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r=9+\\sqrt{221}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"109\" height=\"21\" /> so our answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/0/d/f0d6214f9667cf038ae1f534b985d1d075eb5464.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$9+221=\\boxed{230}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"122\" height=\"23\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_12&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>I would like to use a famous method, namely the coni method. \n</p>\n<pre>Statement .If we consider three complex numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/8/a9874ea56cf8fec1bd246793224fbcfaad17ab3f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A,B,C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> in argand plane then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/d/a/0dafca43354e23a3736f968310d9dcbb44f56c26.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle ABC =\\arg{\\frac{B-A}{B-C}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"162\" height=\"38\" />.\n</pre>\n<p>According to the question given, we can assume ,<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/7/1/e716af84bab8df230a4a0a9f146db1e1467bcd85.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A= f(f(z)),B=f(z),C= z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"236\" height=\"20\" /> respectively.\n</p><p>WLOG,<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/1/f9168bde8bab43af3b29eb89145f73be45df7794.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Z_1= \\frac{f(f(z))-f(z)}{z-f(z)}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"162\" height=\"43\" />.\nAccording to the question <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/8/6/a86438fc4ea6880ec02fe30f32d39dfb1981af14.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\arg{Z_1}=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"90\" height=\"34\" />.\n</p><p>So,<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/f/f7f9c0b0a38a6e7e834719329f2931f75e3ee378.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Re (Z_1)=0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"80\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p><p>Now, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/7/7/8771194cd76758276cf8125c75d6844278746090.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Z_1=\\frac{z(z-19)(z+1)(z-20)}{-z(z-20)}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"238\" height=\"43\" />.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/8/c/58c269406b14fd117d62cf162d81a83d678679a5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\implies Z_1= -(z^2-18z-19)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"222\" height=\"19\" />.\nWLOG,<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/b/d4ba839a7eb3d2c4118e66cc351c0b42526f0064.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z=a+11i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"89\" height=\"14\" /> .where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/9/419339971a455213eb6cff49625b1a0d3287aebb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a=m+\\sqrt{n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"98\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p><p>So,<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/d/f5d394df06d74cb3b4619c79b23364205e375d59.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Re (Z_1)= -(a^2-18a-140)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"214\" height=\"19\" />.\nSolving,<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/4/e04b01048d05d494120d6cc00dd77484324a8505.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a^2-18a-140=0$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"149\" height=\"15\" /> .get ,\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/2/792fa2135a12f746ee8d35f224cc514437dd03c8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a=9$\" width=\"42\" height=\"12\" />±<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/d/a1de7723c27a934a09b96545c82c59726332e083.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{221}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"21\" />.\nSo, possible value of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/9/4c9e84fdbdfe85afde6b5eff6d8a403320ed1b4c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a=9+\\sqrt{221}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"108\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/2/b/22bfe6028ea8907d7de69897eecb26a90d0885e1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m+n=\\boxed{230}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"112\" height=\"23\" />.\n~ftheftics.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Proof_of_this_method\">Proof of this method</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_12&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Proof of this method\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Note that if we translate a triangle, the measures of all of its angles stay the same. So we can translate <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" /> on the complex plane so that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/5/33535d13e10f27c6657f5795ecb46f5c415ad539.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B=0$\" width=\"47\" height=\"12\" />. Let the images of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/9/299ddf7c0f03ea46bf09f81f93adeea0756aa15c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A, B, C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"58\" height=\"16\" /> be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/e/1/ee149d65f55388735f7a78e7410c83ac70dfe67f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A&#39;, B&#39;, C&#39;$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"71\" height=\"17\" /> respectively. Then, we can use the formula:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/5/1/1516a075708b92ab88c8572d17ac159a13030246.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[re^{i\\theta}=r\\cos(\\theta)+i \\cdot r\\sin(\\theta)\\]\" width=\"209\" height=\"20\" /> (This is known as Euler's Formula.)\n</p><p>Using Euler's formula (represent each complex number in polar form, then use exponent identities), we can show that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/7/5976ba13767cf77195ad5293c4b7243a1afbd228.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{arg}(\\frac{A&#39;}{C&#39;})=\\text{arg}(A&#39;)-\\text{arg}(C&#39;)=\\angle(A&#39;B&#39;C&#39;)=\\angle(ABC)\\]\" width=\"418\" height=\"38\" /> So this method is valid.\n~Math4Life2020\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4\">Solution 4</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_12&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>It is well known that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> is perpendicular to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/4/0948661e822f2a953c43a57ac9e40b2734476de4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" /> iff <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/b/3/5b37912a90aa5d2f7c43f9980bf3177c8e93dd8a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{d-c}{b-a}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"42\" height=\"37\" /> is a pure imaginary number. Here, we have that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/e/4ceb9dbc61023ab8da99d371dd2d30a4e7a08ab9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A=z$\" width=\"46\" height=\"13\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/2/5/6255b61f3a040141a0a8d94cd6af1f027c899882.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B,C=f(z)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"94\" height=\"18\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/7/c670506d10fbd20c330bf9aeadbca2dc0bb2375e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D=f(f(z))$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"97\" height=\"18\" />. This means that this is equivalent to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/c/10c37d466ee24c8a67b78778d2c583b122941ce0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{f(f(z))-f(z)}{f(z)-z}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"122\" height=\"45\" /> being a pure imaginary number. Plugging in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/8/8c889ed4d237deb5cec482b543b89306a864a783.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(z)=z^2-19z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"124\" height=\"19\" />, we have that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/4/0147ddb668aec9685c176a81ef2a2124cbb1a693.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{(z^2-19z)-19(z^2-19z)-(z^2-19z)}{z^2-19z-z}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"306\" height=\"39\" /> being pure imaginary. Factoring and simplifying, we find that this is simply equivalent to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/f/33f6f8b7702c39c88c45fecc398b5fa9873aea21.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(z-19)(z+1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"117\" height=\"18\" /> being pure imaginary. We let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/6/b/46b245331cc3cccf7ccaf7e2f6a34fa2131b8b33.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z=a+bi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"79\" height=\"14\" />, so this is equivalent to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/f/6/0f6fac9a395f070c012cac70ad28506ab2579100.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(a+bi-19)(a+bi+1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"190\" height=\"18\" /> being pure imaginary. Expanding the product, this is equivalent to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/5/5/155e7b00688183b39136a6126009c4277db3d45e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a^2+abi+a+abi-b^2+bi-19a-19bi-19$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"358\" height=\"16\" /> being pure imaginary. Taking the real part of this, and setting this equal to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, we have that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/8/d/28d1dbeca16d556da612a98580a39d8b69e6d04c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a^2-18a-b^2-19=0$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"177\" height=\"15\" />. Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/6/3/6636015bc4d4e9e45aee2e6aeda43041a636c64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b=11$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"49\" height=\"13\" />, we have that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/4/e04b01048d05d494120d6cc00dd77484324a8505.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a^2-18a-140=0$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"149\" height=\"15\" />. By the quadratic formula, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/4/7/647ddce4a0512f12638798f0bcb19c93392d22ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a=9 \\pm \\sqrt{221}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"108\" height=\"18\" />, and taking the positive root gives that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/5/905190efa06ebcb6aa44917394f24b82fa46ba45.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a=9+ \\sqrt{221}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"108\" height=\"18\" />, so the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/8/2/6820b155e5908e1d12a6132d551f13eac641fb4c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$9+221=230$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"109\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>~smartninja2000\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_5_.28Official_MAA.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_5_(Official_MAA)\">Solution 5 (Official MAA)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_12&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 5 (Official MAA)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The arguments of the two complex numbers differ by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/2/e/22ec0d2e4202839322d26a23dcb13f79821df5d7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$90^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"28\" height=\"15\" /> if the ratio of the numbers is a pure imaginary number. Thus three distinct complex numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/9/d/b9d05f73e26b6a832d1ea9ca1ce62b44bb1a5806.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A,\\,B,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"43\" height=\"16\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> form a right triangle at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> if and only if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/a/a/daab6fdf43e76a025e1003865cf0e844071c893d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\tfrac{C-B}{B-A}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"33\" height=\"21\" /> has real part equal to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/4/604c735470dd0152ca1cff75fe26eadf5cef7f49.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> Hence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/8/b/f8b1a44bdcd29a40f81f3c891f44118109f2178a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} \\frac{f(f(z))-f(z)}{f(z)-z}&amp;=\\frac{(z^2-19z)^2-19(z^2-19z)-(z^2-19z)}{(z^2-19z)-z}\\\\ &amp;=\\frac{(z^2-19z)(z^2-19z-19-1)}{z^2-20z}\\\\ &amp;=\\frac{z(z-19)(z+1)(z-20)}{z(z-20)} \\\\ &amp;=z^2-18z-19 \\end{align*}\" width=\"456\" height=\"162\" /> must have real part equal to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/4/604c735470dd0152ca1cff75fe26eadf5cef7f49.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/c/55c46fe0d666526aa0e2e906bf7e777fe1cb6869.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z=x+11i,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"94\" height=\"16\" /> the real part of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/3/0730f4e0bfdd9499958bbb9e851d5ff6718f2524.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z^2-18z-19$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"106\" height=\"15\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/b/3/5b3333617e15c7d069b3434ff8ec2fa000f0ec5a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^2-11^2-18x-19,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"160\" height=\"18\" /> which is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> when <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/f/6cf92e1458b06b7d2b090da8b6b06a286439ae1a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=9\\pm\\sqrt{221}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"112\" height=\"18\" /> The requested sum is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/3/8/83826d8c43e23607cad636f1022fb89c5faf248c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$9+221=\\boxed{230}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"128\" height=\"25\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_6_.28Dot_product.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_6_(Dot_product)\">Solution 6 (Dot product)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_12&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 6 (Dot product)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Firstly, the angle between the three complex numbers is equivalent to the angle between <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/b/9/2b971c626d7be99ddd69bb42b6ddbc70694fe6f7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(z)-z,0,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"90\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/8/2f870edaead7fbac48b55166115a61f99f5467fb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(f(z))-f(z)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"118\" height=\"20\" />.\n</p><p>Using <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/2/3/923b5db8d42a4d6cc8e5fc075b8965c6f58926de.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(z)=z(z-19)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"133\" height=\"20\" /> to help expand,\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/3/60361d8f0f8e81fe9f98c4e05ed4fc471567c71b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z-f(z)=20z-z^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"158\" height=\"21\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/8/3/68367a2bcfcf2f7a2092d9cfc2afa6675661cc02.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(f(z))-f(z)=(z^2-19z)(z^2-19z-19)-(z^2-19z)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"446\" height=\"21\" />.\n</p><p>The second equation can be rewritten as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/1/1/e118422842ce982bac048262f4154bce78604916.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(z^2-19z)(z^2-19z-20)=z(z-19)(z-20)(z+1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"419\" height=\"21\" />\n</p><p>Note that the angle between <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/b/36b4b6f79045a8f70af6dcbe3dac1d6385c7c44b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(a+bi), 0,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"84\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/c/f/0cf0ae8226d135df91e0d28fad755aac4830c836.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(a+bi)(c+di)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"122\" height=\"20\" /> is the same as the angle between <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/4/0/0408da06db098bc902e25a04fce022b19b25ccd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1, 0,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"30\" height=\"16\" />and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/9/b/69b5d9cfbac40e0d795398b0648b92ed41ca48ac.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c+di$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"45\" height=\"14\" />.\nThe proof of this is as follows:\n</p><p>Treating the complex numbers like vectors (e.g. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/1/5/615e780fda439b2829922a704a60b31c5ed77191.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a+bi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"45\" height=\"14\" /> turns into <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/5/555efdba31d096a5d5c82299d1ae7af38617cd7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left[\\begin{array}{c}  a \\\\ b \\end{array}\\right]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"47\" height=\"45\" />) we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/2/3b208283ef0febdc615eafa2af811fa1600edf67.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$cos \\theta = \\frac{a \\cdot (ac-bd) + b \\cdot (ad+bc)}{2\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}\\sqrt{c^2+d^2}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"282\" height=\"45\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/d/c/9dc1e4dd9f04f4a42e078f7b9fcf135a3fe251f3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$cos \\theta = \\frac{a^2 c - abd + abd + b^2 c}{2(a^2+b^2)\\sqrt{c^2+d^2}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"233\" height=\"47\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/f/cef3cd1b8e86151e9dc4397f3cedff923164b0b3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$cos \\theta = \\frac{(a^2+b^2)c}{2(a^2+b^2)\\sqrt{c^2+d^2}}=\\frac{c}{2\\sqrt{c^2+d^2}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"319\" height=\"47\" />.\n</p><p>Using the dot product formula for the cosine of the angle between the other two vectors (1, c + di) we get the same result. Thus, it is proven.\n</p><p>Now, we see that the three resulting complex numbers have the same angle as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/c/a3c672c71866c6b1b1e6dc6c8598776244f970a6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-1,0,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"47\" height=\"17\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/f/33f6f8b7702c39c88c45fecc398b5fa9873aea21.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(z-19)(z+1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"117\" height=\"18\" /> as a factor of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/8/3/18349ae081bbb5a7fc84b5bdd35f56741714f6ed.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z(z-20)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"75\" height=\"20\" /> can be taken out of both expressions. Note that the value of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> clearly is not 0, nor is it 20 as it has a value of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/c/90c0a6ac26c417e2217d17f678cb3210ea9f5d83.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$11i$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"23\" height=\"13\" />.\n</p><p>Expanding <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/3/0730f4e0bfdd9499958bbb9e851d5ff6718f2524.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z^2-18z-19$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"106\" height=\"15\" /> with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/b/d4ba839a7eb3d2c4118e66cc351c0b42526f0064.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z=a+11i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"89\" height=\"14\" /> yields\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/c/1/1c1af8c594adc1eb14b3c3c4453f903ea96c837c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a^2-121+22ai-18a-198i-19=a^2-18a-140+22ai-198i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"524\" height=\"18\" />. Turning these into vectors again, the resulting relation is\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/5/1/45161498cff9e8708a2fdcc888493abf0f7c8422.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$cos(90^\\circ)=0=\\frac{-(a^2-18a-140)}{2\\sqrt{(a^2-18a-140)^2 + (22a-198)^2} \\cdot 1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -21px\" width=\"437\" height=\"48\" />\n</p><p>In order for this thing to be 0, the numerator must be 0, so setting it equal to 0 yields\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/7/e/47e4d2795765065ae25c7693155854baff83f250.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a^2-18a-140=0 \\Rightarrow a=9\\pm \\sqrt{81+140}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"328\" height=\"21\" />. Neither of these make the denominator in the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/c/76c0ce695086f31f96bcb6f230e990c218177ff2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$cos(90^\\circ)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"67\" height=\"20\" /> expression 0, so they are valid. The requested result is the positive root, so the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/4/f240441b7decc496acffa34bf690338c99ff43c6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{230}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"40\" height=\"25\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_Omegalearn\">Video Solution by Omegalearn</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_12&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by Omegalearn\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7o6W0F74VOw&amp;list=PLT9bNzqjDoMlmyxKw2bp5nHsEu-Zn9dac&amp;index=58\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7o6W0F74VOw&amp;list=PLT9bNzqjDoMlmyxKw2bp5nHsEu-Zn9dac&amp;index=58</a>\n</p><p>~ Aarav22\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_12&amp;action=edit&amp;section=10\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AIME_I\" title=\"2019 AIME I\">2019 AIME I</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AIME_I_Problems\" title=\"2019 AIME I Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AIME_I_Answer_Key\" title=\"2019 AIME I Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=45&amp;year=2019\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_11\" title=\"2019 AIME I Problems/Problem 11\">Problem 11</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_13\" title=\"2019 AIME I Problems/Problem 13\">Problem 13</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2019 AIME I Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•<